Here is a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::stringstream ss(markdown);
    std::string line;

    while (std::getline(ss, line)) {
        html += "<p>" + line + "</p>";
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello\n\nThis is a test paragraph.\n\nAnother paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a `std::stringstream` to read the markdown text and converts each line to an HTML paragraph (`<p>`). The `std::getline` function is used to read each line of the markdown text, and the resulting HTML paragraph is concatenated to the `html` string. Finally, the function returns the complete HTML string.

Note that this function assumes that the markdown text is well-formed and that each line is separated by a double newline character.

